In 1983, two thousand meters beneath the surface of the Chuquicamata mine, a catastrophic cave-in sealed Shift Six into a lateral drainage pocket. Deprived of radio contact and surviving on compressed air piped through zinc conduits, percussionist-miner Teodoro Choque began tapping the syncopated cumbia tresillo against the conduit walls with a ball-peen hammer to keep the shift awake. The air pipes transformed the strikes into booming, oceanic bass thuds that traveled miles through the rock. Another miner, an ex-naval bandsman named Efraín, assembled his smuggled Bb clarinet and blew long, slow maritime glissandos into the intake grates to soothe the panic, while an electrician wired a salvage guitar to an intercom transformer. When rescuers finally broke through five days later, they found the miners shirtless in silver heat-retardant trousers, covered in blue chalk, playing an impossibly lush, floating dance music that sounded like California surf crashing against the center of the earth. Today, the "Bajosueldo" scene thrives in illegal subterranean mine-raves: dancers wear miner's lamps gelled with sea-foam green film, dance the low-slung paso minero, and share oxygen canisters between songs.

